          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "To solve compound \"and\" inequalities, we solve each inequality separately and then find their intersection.\n\n- First, we solve $-18<10+7z<24\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-18 & <10+7z<24 \\\\ -18-10 & <10+7z-10<24-10 \\\\ -28 & <7z<14 \\\\ \\frac{-28}{7} & <\\frac{7z}{7}<\\frac{14}{7} \\\\ -4 & <z<2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255803/assets/778e9b621c822549cdd15f68553596e6a4a53026588ad9832fdb251e7e851b02.png)\n\n- Then, we solve $3z\\ge -9\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z & \\ge -9 \\\\ \\frac{3z}{3} & \\ge \\frac{-9}{3} \\\\ z & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14255803/assets/ad16a2201986ec6531d6dca654b74ca10f34bf6859e0275aea472364b7262800.png)\n\nTo find the values of $z$ that satisfy both inequalities, we find where the two solutions overlap:\n\n![](@asset-2@)\n\nThus, the final solution is $-3\\le z<2.$",
